  "yesterday_verification_summary": "For yesterday (2026-10-09) at Day+0, all models correctly verified rain, though ICON and Best Match captured onset timing with zero error while GFS, ECMWF, and UKMO lagged significantly. Day+3 verification for 2026-10-06 saw all models correctly forecast rainfall. At Day+7 (2026-10-02), ECMWF and Best Match verified rain calls correctly, whereas GFS produced a false alarm.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Today's Forecast\nHigh temperatures reaching 29.6°C / 85°F under mostly cloudy skies early, clearing to mostly clear by midday before turning mostly cloudy into the evening. Afternoon showers and thunderstorms are expected, with rain onset from 16:00 and total rainfall near 6.9 mm (0.27 in). Thunder possible from the morning, peaking this afternoon. Winds will be northeasterly overnight, turning southwest by midday and west-southwest into the evening, with peak gusts up to 38.9 km/h (21 kt). Solar UV exposure will reach a peak UV index of 9.5 around midday. Overnight temperatures drop to a low of 19.6°C / 67°F. Ground air quality ranges from an AQI of 36 to 121 (Moderate to USG), with the worst reading at Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central. As of 06:01, reports through 05:00 show no rain, no thunder, a high so far of 21°C / 70°F, low so far of 19°C / 66°F, peak sustained winds of 7 km/h (4 kt), and 2/8 cloud coverage.\n\n## Extended Outlook\nTemperatures and winds much the same through Tuesday, with showers and thunderstorms likely each day. Over the next three days, Sunday and Monday will bring mostly cloudy conditions before shifting to partly cloudy on Tuesday, with daily high temperatures on Tuesday spanning a range of 29°C / 84°F to 33°C / 91°F. Into the extended range from Wednesday through Saturday, mostly cloudy skies will dominate, with continued opportunities for showers and afternoon thunderstorms supported by persisting convective instability. Rain probabilities remain elevated throughout the week, with total daily rainfall generally ranging between 1 mm and 10 mm.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nConvective instability poses a distinct thunderstorm hazard this afternoon and evening. Model predictions for peak CAPE vary significantly: GFS shows modest instability capping at 360 J/kg, while ECMWF (1490 J/kg) and ICON (1740 J/kg) show moderate-to-high instability, and UKMO projects strong potential reaching 3780 J/kg. Any thunderstorm developing in this environment can generate sudden, violent downburst gusts far in excess of background sustained winds, posing immediate risks on land and exposed waters.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\nOver open waters of Winam Gulf, expect winds to be east-northeasterly overnight at 5 km/h (3 kt) gusting 7 km/h (4 kt), then southwesterly by midday at 9 km/h (5 kt) gusting 19 km/h (10 kt), then west-southwesterly into the evening at 7 km/h (4 kt) gusting 17 km/h (9 kt). Peak background gusts over the gulf will reach 28.4 km/h (15 kt). However, with thunderstorm onset expected around 16:00, boaters should prepare for sudden convective wind shifts and dangerous localized squalls capable of producing sudden high gusts on the lake.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n### Synoptic Overview\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the north (1007 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1015 hPa) — an 8 hPa spread, a moderate large-scale gradient. The lower pressure to the north is filling over the next three days, while pressure overhead remains near-steady. Locally across Nyanza Basin, mean sea-level pressure ranges from 1012.6 hPa at Kisumu to 1014.7 hPa at higher southern basin points, maintaining a modest regional pressure gradient that supports the development of afternoon lake breezes.\n\nGround air quality monitoring across three reporting stations shows local variability across Kisumu. Kisumu Airport reports an AQI of 36 (Good, PM2.5 36.0 µg/m³), Dunga Beach reports an AQI of 52 (Moderate, PM2.5 52.0 µg/m³), and Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central measures an AQI of 121 (Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups, PM2.5 121.0 µg/m³), all recorded 3.0 hours ago. CAMS model guidance aligns well with broad background levels, forecasting an overall regional US AQI around 58 to 66.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\nData sufficiency at Day+0 represents a settled record with 46 to 59 verified checks per model. Today's forecaster call for a high of 29.6°C / 85°F sits between GFS's warm call of 31.9°C and ECMWF's cool call of 28.7°C, aligning with long-run review findings that GFS systematically over-forecasts daytime highs while ECMWF runs slightly cool. For overnight lows, ICON projects the warmest low at 21.9°C, consistent with its established bias of over-forecasting overnight temperatures, whereas UKMO calls the coolest low at 18.4°C, reflecting its tendency to under-forecast night temperatures.\n\nKenya Meteorological Department (KMD) local bulletin was unavailable this run. In the historical record, Kenya Met exhibits a 60.0% 10-check rain accuracy at Day+0 with a slight warm bias on overnight lows, performing similarly to global numerical guidance.",
